==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|hurtfull}} {{qualifier|archaic}}

===Etymology===
{{suffix|hurt|ful|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Tending to [[impair]] or [[damage]]; injurious; [[occasion]]ing [[loss]] or [[injury]].
#* '''1649''': [[w:John Milton|John Milton]], ''Eikonoklastes''
#*: A good principle not rightly understood may prove as '''hurtful''' as a bad.
#* '''1890''': George Henry Rohé, ''Text-book of hygiene''
#*: Well-cultivated soils are often healthy; nor at present has it been proved that the use of manure is '''hurtful'''.
# Tending to [[hurt someone's feelings]]; [[insulting]].
#* {{quote-book|year=2000|title=Violent No More|author=Michael Paymar|passage=Both men and women can be emotionally abusive. Even in the healthiest relationships, people occasionally reach down into their personal bags of known remembrances, past disagreements, and unresolved issues, and fling hurtful comments at their partners.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2006|title=Fall from Grace|author=Ryan Phillips|passage=Better yet, maybe she should call and apologize for all of the rude comments she spewed in the midst of her anger—hurtful comments that should never be spoken between a wife and her husband.}}

